From 94422f671f2f1df249ca2eddccce6afabd0c8c43 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 19:38:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Film buffer sized to the TV's memory, no 4K skip preview on
 small TVs, libass overlay only when used

Fix 3: the film's buffer goes by the TV's memory in all (ActivityManager
totalMem and isLowRamDevice), not the app's heap limit. FilmMemory/RamTier
pick the tier: low-RAM or under 1.4 GiB 64 MiB with 4 s kept behind; under
1.8 GiB 128 MiB / 6 s (the Fire TV Stick 4K Max); under 2.6 GiB 176 MiB /
10 s; otherwise 256 MiB / 15 s. 0.6 x maxMemory and 400 MiB stay as upper
caps, 48 MiB is the floor. FilmLoadControl owns the DefaultAllocator and
can be squeezed at run time (PlayerFactory.Built.squeeze): step 1 fills to
3/4, step 2 to 1/2 (never under 48 MiB), with the allocator trimmed; it
never stops loading with under 5 s ahead, so start, stall and cushion
rules are unchanged. PlayerActivity squeezes on onTrimMemory
(RUNNING_LOW step 1, RUNNING_CRITICAL step 2) and on a 10 s MemoryInfo
check in the play tick (low: step 1, still low 30 s later: step 2), and
gives a step back after 2 minutes without pressure. The live player is
unchanged.

Fix 5: on TVs under 2.6 GiB the skip preview no longer goes past its
1080p limit, so a 4K-only film gets no second 4K decoder; SeekPreview says
so once through its failure path and skipping works as without previews.
Its player is let go 3 s after skipping stops there (15 s elsewhere).

Fix 8: libass's OpenGL overlay (TextureView, render thread, GL context)
is attached only the first time an ASS/SSA track is chosen, instead of
for every film; parsing and libass's renderer are set up as before, and
"Keep embedded styling" means what it did.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
